<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Kazallu</h1><p class="st-meta">1900 BCE – 1800 BCE (approx.) · capital Kazallu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Amorite-era city-kingdom of northern Babylonia, an early rival of Babylon whose walls were torn down by Sumu-la-El.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Kazallu lay in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marad">Marad</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kish">Kish</a>; its site has not been found. Under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire</a> it was a province, and its governor Puzur-Numushda defied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibbi-sin">Ibbi-Sin</a> in the last years of Ur. In the 19th century BCE it became the seat of an independent kingdom tied to the Amorite Mutiabal tribe.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kazallu is known mainly through the year names and inscriptions of its neighbours, which record wars and alliances with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/larsa">Larsa</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/isin">Isin</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/uruk">Uruk</a> and the new Amorite dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. Kings such as Yahzir-El briefly controlled the region, and the city's position on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> canals gave it influence over northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia's</a> grain lands. Its own institutions are almost undocumented.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumu-la-el">Sumu-la-El</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> defeated Kazallu and destroyed its walls in the early 19th century BCE, and it was absorbed by Babylon. Its Mutiabal population later rose against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a>, but by about 1800 BCE the kingdom had ceased to exist.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1900 BCE – 1800 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesopotamia and Iran</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:21.89%;width:1.99%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1900 BCE – 1800 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kazallu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Yahzir-El</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mesopotamian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Akkadian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Iraq</dd></dl></aside></div>

